    "capturedAt": "2026-07-02",
    "content": "# Workshop 05 - Customer Experience and Accessibility\n\nDate: 2 July 2026. Facilitator: Priya Raghavan. Transcribed automatically.\n\nAISHA BELL (Customer Experience Lead, Meridian Bank): The number that keeps me\nawake is 61% abandonment. When we interviewed people who dropped out, the two\nwords that came back were \"slow\" and \"confusing\".\n\nPRIYA RAGHAVAN (Engagement Lead, Northgate Advisory): So what does good look\nlike?\n\nAISHA BELL: The application must have fast response time so customers do not\nabandon the journey. That is the headline.\n\nPRIYA RAGHAVAN: Can we put a number on fast?\n\nAISHA BELL: I would rather not commit to a number in the room. It needs to feel\ninstant. Engineering can tell us what is achievable.\n\nAISHA BELL: The second one is comprehension. The interface should be easy to use\nfor customers who are not comfortable with digital banking. A meaningful share of\nour applicants are over sixty.\n\nMARCUS TRAN (Branch Operations Manager, Meridian Bank): We see that in branch\nconstantly. People give up at the income and employment section.\n\nAISHA BELL: Right. So the system must explain in plain language why each piece of\ninformation is being requested.\n\nPRIYA RAGHAVAN: Accessibility standard?\n\nAISHA BELL: This one I can be precise about because it is a bank policy. The\nonboarding journey must conform to WCAG 2.2 Level AA. That is non-negotiable, it\nis in our accessibility policy and we have been audited against it before.\n\nAISHA BELL: And the journey must be available in English and Welsh.\n\nPRIYA RAGHAVAN: Welsh because of the branch footprint?\n\nAISHA BELL: Statutory. We have branches in Wales.\n\nAISHA BELL: Last one from me. If the customer gets stuck, the system should offer\na handover to a human adviser without losing the data already entered.\n\nMARCUS TRAN: That is a big one for us. Today the handover means starting again.\n\nAISHA BELL: And it needs to be a good experience, not a jarring one. The design\nshould feel modern and reassuring.\n\nPRIYA RAGHAVAN: I will capture that as a design principle rather than a\nrequirement, it is not testable as written.\n"
